{{- /* Who git trusts to have signed a commit.

       Two entries, not one, and the second is why this stopped being a
       one-liner: a rotated signing key must not silently turn every commit it
       already signed into an unverifiable one. git checks a signature against
       this file using the *commit's* timestamp, so a retired key bounded with
       valid-before keeps verifying its own history while being unable to
       vouch for anything dated after the boundary.

       The current key still comes from the vault through signing-pubkey, so a
       rotation is one edit there plus one entry here. */ -}}
{{- /* The retired key, retired for a reason worth writing down.

       Its private half is in the public history of this repository, in
       private_dot_ssh/encrypted_private_id_ed25519_signing.age — age-encrypted,
       but only to an identity whose own private half (key.txt.age) sat at the
       root of the same public repo behind a single passphrase. It signed
       everything up to the date below and should be trusted for exactly that.

       valid-before is the whole point, not a courtesy: without it this entry
       would let anyone who recovers that key sign a commit today and have git
       call it verified. */ -}}
{{- $retiredKey := "ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIMj1CJnE/kvOEVs8B8AWfDSKAtICy8fP45R0QYAZdhbf" -}}
{{- /* A week out from when this was written, not the day of. The retired key
       is still the one in the vault until the rotation is finished, so a
       boundary of "today" would mean any commit made between merging this and
       swapping the vault item fails to verify. A week is grace, not a target:
       move it to the day the rotation actually completes. */ -}}
{{- $retiredBefore := "20260811Z" -}}
{{ .email }} valid-before="{{ $retiredBefore }}" {{ $retiredKey }}
{{- $key := includeTemplate "signing-pubkey" . }}
{{- if $key }}
{{- /* While the vault still holds the retired key — the window between merging
       this and finishing the rotation — signing-pubkey returns exactly the
       key above, and emitting it again unbounded would hand back the forgery
       window the line above just closed. So it is skipped, and commits made in
       that window are the ones to make after the vault swap, not before.

       Compared on the key material alone, never on the whole line. The two
       sources disagree about the trailing comment: the agent fallback strips
       it (`print $1" "$2`) and the vault field carries it, so the vault path
       returned "ssh-ed25519 AAAA… git-signing" against a bare $retiredKey and
       `ne` was true no matter what. That is not a cosmetic mismatch — it
       emitted the retired key a second time with no valid-before, which is
       exactly the forgery window this guard exists to close, and it did so
       silently. Found 28 August 2026. */}}
{{- $bare := $key | splitList " " -}}
{{- if ge (len $bare) 2 -}}
{{- $bare = printf "%s %s" (index $bare 0) (index $bare 1) -}}
{{- else -}}
{{- $bare = $key -}}
{{- end }}
{{- if ne $bare $retiredKey }}
{{ .email }} {{ $key }}
{{- end }}
{{- end }}
